            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Frank Lloyd Wright's Early Life</strong><br>\nFrank Lloyd Wright was born in 1867 in Wisconsin, USA. From an early age, he showed a keen interest in buildings and the natural environment. Wright was admitted to the University of Wisconsin-Madison, where he studied engineering because they offered no architecture degree. He left the university without finishing his degree to work for an architect in Chicago.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Influences</strong><br>\nIn Chicago, Wright worked under architect Louis Sullivan, who was known as the 'father of skyscrapers'. Sullivan's belief that a building's design should reflect its purpose greatly influenced Wright. This principle later evolved into Wright's own philosophy of \"organic architecture,\" which promoted harmony between human habitation and the natural world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Prairie School</strong><br>\nWright established his own architectural practice in 1893. He became known for designing structures that were in harmony with their environment, a style that became known as the Prairie School. His designs featured low-pitched roofs, overhanging eaves, and a central hearth, reflecting the flat, expansive landscape of the American Midwest.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Innovative Design Techniques</strong><br>\nWright was not only known for his distinctive style but also for his innovative design techniques. He often designed custom furniture and fixtures for his buildings and experimented with new building materials and methods. He also developed the concept of the 'Usonian' home, which was a model for affordable and efficient housing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Famous Works</strong><br>\nAmong Wright's most famous works are the Robie House in Chicago and the Imperial Hotel in Tokyo. His design for the Fallingwater house in Pennsylvania, which extends over a waterfall, is especially renowned. It is considered one of the most beautiful residences ever built and a supreme example of Wright's integration of architecture with nature.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Challenges</strong><br>\nWright's personal life was often controversial, marked by public scandals, debts, and tumultuous relationships. Despite these challenges, he continued to produce a vast array of innovative designs. Wright's personal experiences, including tragedies such as the 1914 fire at his Taliesin studio, deeply affected his life and work.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
