          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Wright's Philosophy</strong><br>\nWright's architectural philosophy was centered on the idea that buildings should serve to enhance and reflect the beauty of their natural surroundings. He believed in designing structures that were not just functional but also spiritually uplifting, creating a sense of peace and unity for the inhabitants.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Contributions</strong><br>\nWright's contributions to architecture were not limited to his designs. He was also an influential educator, founding the Taliesin Fellowship, a training program for architects that combined hands-on construction work with study. His ideas and teachings have left a lasting impact on the field of architecture.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Recognition</strong><br>\nFrank Lloyd Wright passed away in 1959, but his legacy endures. Many of his buildings are listed as National Historic Landmarks, and he has been recognized by the American Institute of Architects as \"the greatest American architect of all time.\" His innovative designs and educational endeavors continue to influence architects worldwide.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
